//? Title: Power divergence and trend in proportions
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

The Cressie–Read family compares observed category counts with specified
expected counts. Changing its power recovers the familiar Pearson or
likelihood-ratio statistic. When categories are ordered, Cochran–Armitage
instead tests a directional association with supplied category scores.

```{cpp, id=categorical-tests, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/chi_squared_test.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/proportion_test.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    std::println("| Power | Statistic | p-value |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const double power : {1., 0., -1.}) {
        const auto fit = power_divergence_test({25, 15}, {.5, .5}, power);
        std::println("| {:.0f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", power, fit.statistic, fit.p_value);
    }
    const auto trend = cochran_armitage_test(
        {2, 5, 8}, {10, 10, 10}, {0, 1, 2}, Alternative::Greater);
    std::println("\nFor observed proportions 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8, the trend test gives "
                 "z = {:.3f} and one-sided p = {:.5f}.", trend.statistic, trend.p_value);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Power | Statistic | p-value |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 2.5000 | 0.1138 |
| 0 | 2.5267 | 0.1119 |
| -1 | 2.5815 | 0.1081 |

For observed proportions 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8, the trend test gives z = 2.683 and one-sided p = 0.00365.
// result_end
